## Why This Is Asked
Tests for per-node caches in multi-node services are common in high-scale organizations. A beginner-friendly test plan demonstrates understanding of TTL eviction, cross-node consistency via source-of-truth invalidation, and stampede protection without overengineering.

## Key Concepts
- **Cache TTL and eviction**: Time-based expiration ensures stale data is removed
- **Per-node isolation vs. cross-node consistency**: Each node maintains its own cache; invalidation must propagate to all nodes
- **Cache stampede and coalescing**: Prevents thundering herd when cache misses occur simultaneously
- **Time manipulation in CI**: Essential for testing TTL behavior without waiting in tests
- **Burst-load testing**: Simulates real traffic spikes to verify cache behavior under pressure

## Code Example
```javascript
class Cache {
  constructor(ttlMs) {
    this.ttl = ttlMs;
    this.map = new Map();
  }

  set(k, v) {
    this.map.set(k, { v, exp: Date.now() + this.ttl });
  }

  get(k) {
    const entry = this.map.get(k);
    if (!entry) return null;
    if (Date.now() > entry.exp) {
      this.map.delete(k);
      return null;
    }
    return entry.v;
  }
}```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of retry, DLQ, and idempotency in a queue-based worker.

## Key Concepts
- Retry policies and backoff
- DLQ handling
- Idempotency and dedup
- Test doubles for queue and worker
- Time manipulation in tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test pseudo
test('DLQ after 3 retries', async () => {
  const queue = new RedisQueueMock();
  queue.enqueue({jobId:'abc', image:'data'});
  const worker = makeWorker(queue, {maxRetries:3});
  await simulateFailures(worker, 'first');
  await worker.processOne();
  expect(queue.hasDLQ('abc')).toBe(true);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate time so backoff timing doesn't slow tests?
- How would you ensure DLQ content includes error type and original payload?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical end-to-end testing of a distributed MongoDB search feature, focusing on ranking accuracy, fuzziness robustness, pagination, performance KPIs, and resilience under failover and repartitioning. It also emphasizes realistic CI integration and data generation strategies.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end search tests for ranking and fuzziness
- Performance metrics: p95 latency, throughput, error rate
- Resilience: replica failover, shard migration, partitions
- Data modeling: synthetic boosts and realistic term distributions
- CI data management and flaky-test handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test sketch (pseudo)
async function testSearchRanking() {
  const res = await api.search('coffee', { fuzziness: 1, page: 1, perPage: 10 })
  if (res.results.length !== 10) throw new Error('unexpected length')
  if (res.results[0].score < res.results[1].score) throw new Error('ranking not sorted')
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ranking under load spikes and index churn?
- How would you detect and isolate flaky tests caused by replication lag?

